Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern used to make code more flexible and testable. Instead of an object creating its own dependencies, the dependencies are "injected" from an outside source.

This pattern is a form of **Inversion of Control (IoC)**, where the control of creating dependencies is shifted from the object itself to an external entity.

### How it Works

Imagine you have a `Car` class. A car needs an `Engine` to function.

**Without Dependency Injection**, the `Car` class would create its own `Engine` instance inside its constructor.

```javascript
class Engine {
  start() {
    console.log("Engine started");
  }
}

class Car {
  constructor() {
    this.engine = new Engine(); // Car is creating its own dependency
  }

  start() {
    this.engine.start();
    console.log("Car has started");
  }
}

const myCar = new Car();
myCar.start();
```

The problem here is that the `Car` class is tightly coupled to the `Engine` class. You can't easily swap in a different type of engine (like an `ElectricEngine`) without changing the `Car` class itself. This also makes testing difficult, as you can't provide a "mock" engine for tests.

**With Dependency Injection**, the `Engine` is created outside the `Car` and passed into its constructor.

```javascript
class V8Engine {
  start() {
    console.log("V8 engine started");
  }
}

class ElectricEngine {
    start() {
        console.log("Electric engine started... quietly");
    }
}

class Car {
  constructor(engine) { // The 'engine' is injected
    this.engine = engine;
  }

  start() {
    this.engine.start();
    console.log("Car has started");
  }
}

// The dependency is created outside and "injected"
const v8 = new V8Engine();
const myCar = new Car(v8);
myCar.start();

const electricEngine = new ElectricEngine();
const myTesla = new Car(electricEngine);
myTesla.start();
```

Now, the `Car` class isn't responsible for creating its engine. It just needs *an* engine that has a `start` method. This makes the `Car` class more flexible, reusable, and much easier to test.
